WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.320
Before a fat can fuel anything, it has to be unpacked in three deliberate stages.

2
00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:13.760
First, the triacylglycerols stored in adipose tissue are hydrolyzed by lipase,

3
00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:19.100
splitting each molecule into three free fatty acids plus a glycerol backbone, which

4
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:21.860
travel out into the blood toward tissues that need energy.

5
00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:27.860
Second, fatty acids are activated; long chains are shuttled into the mitochondria.

6
00:00:28.600 --> 00:00:34.120
Third, inside the matrix, the fatty acid is dismantled into acetyl-CoA units the

7
00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:35.640
citric acid cycle can burn.

8
00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:37.880
Keep this roadmap in mind.

9
00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:41.620
Release, activation and import, then degradation.

10
00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:45.780
Everything that follows is just detail filling in these three moves.

11
00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:49.760
Long-chain acyl-CoA faces a wall.

12
00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:54.280
It cannot cross the inner mitochondrial membrane, so the acyl group needs a

13
00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:55.900
different passport to reach the matrix.

14
00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:58.200
That passport is carnitine.

15
00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:03.620
In step one, the fatty acid is activated to acyl CoA in the cytosol.

16
00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:09.160
Then carnitine palmitoyl transferase I swaps CoA for carnitine forming

17
00:01:09.160 --> 00:01:13.300
acyl-carnitine, which the translocase ferries across the inner membrane.

18
00:01:14.020 --> 00:01:19.900
On the matrix side, CPT II reverses the swap, handing the acyl group back to a fresh

19
00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:22.780
CoA so beta oxidation can begin.

20
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.860
Carnitine itself is recycled back out.

21
00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:29.980
This shuttle is a key checkpoint controlling entry of long chains.

22
00:01:31.083 --> 00:01:34.963
The name beta-oxidation tells you exactly where the chemistry happens.

23
00:01:35.643 --> 00:01:38.783
Number the carbons starting from the carboxyl carbon as carbon 1.

24
00:01:39.563 --> 00:01:44.723
Carbon 2 is the alpha carbon, and carbon 3, the next one out, is the beta carbon.

25
00:01:45.523 --> 00:01:50.663
Beta-oxidation means we attack and oxidize that beta carbon, converting it to a keto

26
00:01:50.663 --> 00:01:52.583
group so the chain can be cleaved there.

27
00:01:53.223 --> 00:01:55.743
The delta notation is a companion convention.

28
00:01:56.923 --> 00:02:01.563
Delta N marks the first carbon of a double bond, so delta 2 means a double bond

29
00:02:01.563 --> 00:02:03.143
between carbons 2 and 3.

30
00:02:03.963 --> 00:02:09.763
Each round of oxidation generates acetyl-CoA, along with the reduced carriers NADH

31
00:02:09.763 --> 00:02:13.883
and FADH2, the energy currency we are after.

32
00:02:14.933 --> 00:02:19.773
Let's tally the payoff for fully oxidizing palmitate, a 16-carbon fat.

33
00:02:20.753 --> 00:02:25.093
Activation costs two ATP equivalents up front, so we start at minus 2.

34
00:02:26.073 --> 00:02:32.413
Seven rounds of beta-oxidation each yield 1 FADH2 and 1 NADH, and those seven rounds

35
00:02:32.413 --> 00:02:34.433
produce eight acetyl-CoA.

36
00:02:34.433 --> 00:02:38.493
Feed all eight through the citric acid cycle and run the reduced carriers through

37
00:02:38.493 --> 00:02:43.233
oxidative phosphorylation, and the books close at roughly 106 ATP.

38
00:02:43.233 --> 00:02:48.053
Compare that to glucose, which nets around 32 ATP per molecule.

39
00:02:48.793 --> 00:02:52.833
Per carbon, fat yields about 25% % more ATP than sugar.

40
00:02:53.733 --> 00:02:58.993
That density is exactly why the body stores long-term energy as fat rather than

41
00:02:58.993 --> 00:02:59.473
carbohydrate.
